  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 348 AM CDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nA wet and cooler weather pattern takes shape beginning Monday night \nlasting through at least the middle part of the work week. This is \nthanks to remnants of Polo and an upper-level storm system moving \nnear the region. Long-range guidance has the remnants of Polo being \nabsorbed into the upper-level storm system. This will provide \nabundant mid-level Pacific moisture while lee cyclogenesis draws in \nGulf moisture at the lower levels. Areawide rain chances are \nexpected by late Monday night lasting through Wednesday. Rain will \nbegin across the western portions of the forecast area Monday night \nand shift eastward heading into Tuesday. Guidance has indicated \nTuesday will be the \"rainiest\" day with abundant moisture from \nboth features. Ensemble guidance also suggests PWATs reaching near\nand even exceeding daily maximum climatological values throughout\nTuesday. Given these anomalous PWATs, heavy rainfall leading to \nflash flooding is going to be the main concern. Long-range \nensemble and deterministic models are trending towards the highest\nrainfall amounts remaining along and north of the Pecos River, \nwith the exception of locations in Culberson County. Uncertainty \nstill exists in exact amounts, timing, and impacts given any minor\nshift in both storm systems will alter potential impacts. We will\ncontinue to monitor trends closely. \n\nBesides the rain, a cold front is forecast to move through the \nregion sometime on Wednesday/Wednesday night bringing cooler \ntemperatures in the upper 70s to mid 80s. Drier conditions and \nslightly cooler temperatures (mid 70s to low 80s) are expected \nbehind the front Thursday and Friday as surface high pressure builds \nin from the north. Moisture wrapping around the surface low will \nprovide low chances (10-30%) of rainfall across the eastern half of \nthe area late week. Temperatures look to stay near normal for early \nOctober standards being in the mid 70s to low 80s by next weekend. \n\nLamberson",
